A||||8||westtexas||Turbo 4.9 brainstorming||||1||
Z||000000||westtexas||11-21-2001||09:22 AM||||The Cad 4.9 is mostly torque and some  horsepower. On the plus side the junkyard situation will get better everyday. Old Cadillacs have complicated systems that cost more to fix then they are worth. Basically, for prestige, a '91-92 cad is a has-been car. A lot of these engines are driven by little old ladies and are low mileage.<P>1. 300 ft-lbs will get you off the line, turbo lag shouldn't be a problem. <P>2. The rpm range is approx. 1000 to 4500 or  3500 RPMS.<P>3. Turbo's spool up to the square of the engine speed; engines with 6,000 rpm ranges are harder to turbo. Torque without lag and horsepower without exhaust restriction on the top end takes some sophisticated engineering.<P>4. With it's limited rpm range, It seems to me that the cad was designed for an automatic and should be used with one. Automatics are also handy for spooling up a turbo.<P> So, what's wrong with this idea? Use a small turbo on each exhaust side designed for approx. 5 to 6 pounds boost max from 3,000 to 4,500. This should give another 70 hp making the 4.9 competitive with the 3800 II SC but much cheaper. <P>I had a friend 25 years ago who put a Cat heavy equipment turbo on a Rambler six. It was purely backyard engineering using a carb and a standard distributor (this was during the stone age). He had a blast beating big V-8 sedans in street races. He was boosting more than 10 pounds. His little sixes came and went but replacements from the junkyard were only $100. This idea is for just having some cheap street fun with a Fiero, not for daily drivers that have to reliable. Might get to know your friendly tow truck driver well.<P>Anyway, thanks for little me air this idea.<P>Robert <p>[This message has been edited by westtexas (edited 11-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||11-21-2001||10:18 AM||||one from a 301 turbo trans-am? about the same engine size and rpm range. i need a stick though.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MinnGreenGT||11-21-2001||10:23 AM||||I certainly like the idea... can you rig the computer to compensate for the turbo (added fuel to compensate)?  Or is there an aftermarket computer that'll do it?  Need some form of a rising-rate fuel pressure regulator.  Anyway- I think it'd be an awsome ride!  Go for it and let us know how it works out.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hartz||11-21-2001||11:02 AM||||Sounds like a neat project.  My only concern would be heat management.  Two turbos and a V8 are going to make things mighty toasty back there.  Ducting cool air from some scoops, having a rear decklid scoop, or somehow rigging an intercooler into the mix, along with adding a massive new radiator should help, but I suspect it wouldn't be easy.  <P>Best of luck with it.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com||||reg||7
Z||000004||westtexas||11-21-2001||11:06 AM||||Rob, <P>I have to admit I very ignorant about fuel injection and fuel management. I probably will be for long while. Rockcrawl on the other hand knows what he is talking about. If he should ever go commercial we would all benefit. On another Forum he mentioned using a '94 Cad computer with the 4.9. If he wrote a book called Cad Computers for Dummies I would buy the first ten copies. Assuming I could get that to work and use a rising rate fuel pump, then that could be a decent solution. Thanks for the idea. Perhaps water injection would be a good, cheap way to keep knock down also. But my main principle will be to go as cheap as possible using salvage yard parts. I love the sound of a LT1 ripping asphalt off the road but... I'm a lot short several thousand, besides that would be a sure thing. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||westtexas||11-21-2001||11:27 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Hartz:<BR>[B]Sounds like a neat project.  My only concern would be heat management.  <P>Hartz, <P>Yes, heat management will be a problem. My brain is no longer fully functional. I had thought of that and for that reason dismissed the idea of turboing. Then I thunk about supercharging but the last thing a 4.9 needs is more torque and the manifold/drive system is over my head. I'm just a shade tree without lots of money to hire professionals to get it 'done right'. I read a post some moons back from a winner who put $10,000 in a 2.8 to make 275 HP at 7,000 RPM. He's my hero. I'll take your advice on this. A massive radiator will be included. If air/liquid intercoolers are cheap, I'll use those too. Obviously, this may not be pretty. Perhaps I can build scoops/thermostatically controlled fans into the decklid. Thanks for you help!<P>Robert<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||MinnGreenGT||11-21-2001||12:54 PM||||Heat <I>could</I> be an issue... but that's easily solved.  Pick up a mid-80's 'stang scoop from a junkyard (or $80 from Ferrari_Momo) to vent the decklid.  Maybe even add a few electric fans under the existing vents (I was thinking of doing this to my GT w/3.1 anyway).  Also adding a set of "pontiac" or "mecham" side scoops, bringing more fresh air in would help.  And the radiator is definitely a good way to go!  The intercooler woudn't necessarily decrease "under-hood" temps, but it would certainly make the turbo much safer at a higher boost.  Keep in mind that an intercooler itself needs airflow (works like a radiator)...  I'm still liking the idea overall though!  Keep it rolling.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lou_dias||11-23-2001||09:29 PM||||Don't get side scoops unless you like replacing alternators once a year.  Too much dirt gets into the engine compartment.  I'm sure the aluminum block and intake of the Caddy with a 4 core radiator and a 180 stat will be just fine.<P>I have Mecham scoops on my 87 GT.  Slight crack on one of the fins, easily fixed (I was bumped into a wall at Seekonk Speedway).  I'd consider going back to stock if I hadn't thrown out my original panels.||||reg||1
Z||000008||lou_dias||11-23-2001||09:32 PM||||forgot that the point was to cool a turbo...<BR>ok, make sure you mount the intercooler under the battery and infront of the wheel and try to seal it from the engine compartment.  Maybe cut some holes in the wheelwell mud guard so that it doubles as airflow to your rear brakes.||||reg||1
